challenges.description:
TypeScript's type system is erased at runtime. Given this:

  interface Config { host: string; port: number }
  const config: Config = JSON.parse(process.env.CONFIG!)

What problem does this create even though it compiles?

Which of these is the correct pattern for a type guard that validates an unknown value as a Config?

  A)  function isConfig(v: any): v is Config { return true }
  B)  function isConfig(v: unknown): v is Config { return typeof v === 'object' && v !== null && 'host' in v && 'port' in v }
  C)  function isConfig(v: unknown): Config { return v as Config }
  D)  function isConfig(v: Config): boolean { return !!v }
